  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x-3 & =-({x}^{2}+4x+3) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+[{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}]+3) \\\\  & =-((x+2)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =1-(x+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x+2.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-5/2$ | $-3/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1/2$ | $1/2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x-3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5/2}^{-3/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x+2)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061u\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
